To integrate the read-through S3 cache natively into the Wintersys Agile Deployment Toolkit (ADT), we replace direct aws CLI calls with ADT's built-in datastore abstraction scripts (MountDatastore.sh, GetFromDatastore.sh, and PutToDatastore.sh) located under ${HOME}/services/datastore/operations/ (or ${BUILD_HOME}/services/datastore/operations/ on the build machine).
This makes your nala cache automatically inherit ADT's provider-agnostic S3 tooling (s3cmd, s5cmd, or rclone) as well as its multi-region replication chains (S3_HOST_BASE / S3_ACCESS_KEY pipe-delimited configs). [1] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/Development/AddingANewDatastoreProvider)

ADT Integration Architecture

1. Bucket Nomenclature: Following ADT's datastore naming conventions (<website_url>-<purpose> or <prefix>-apt-cache), we isolate packages by OS codename and architecture (e.g., ${CACHE_BUCKET}/debian-bookworm-amd64/) so mixed fleets never collide.
2. Region Awareness:
	• MountDatastore.sh: Invoked with distributed mode so the cache bucket is provisioned across all configured regions/providers in your ADT template.
	• GetFromDatastore.sh: Invoked against the local region (local / index 1 in ADT's credential chain) for fast, low-latency pulls.
	• PutToDatastore.sh: Invoked with distributed mode (or local if you only want single-region caching) so newly fetched .deb packages propagate across all configured regions.

Refactored ADT Script: ${HOME}/utilities/software/InstallWithNalaCache.sh

Save this script in your forked ADT repository (e.g., under utilities/software/ across your webserver, database, autoscaler, and build-machine script trees). [1] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/Development/AddingANewDatastoreProvider)
sh
#!/bin/sh
###############################################################################################
# Description: Read-through S3 package cache wrapper for nala integrated with Wintersys ADT
# Author: Refactored for Wintersys Agile Deployment Toolkit
###############################################################################################
set -eu

# 1. Resolve ADT base directory (${HOME} on provisioned nodes, ${BUILD_HOME} on build machine)
ADT_BASE="${BUILD_HOME:-${HOME}}"
OPS_DIR="${ADT_BASE}/services/datastore/operations"

# Verify ADT datastore scripts exist; fallback cleanly to plain nala if not yet initialised
if [ ! -x "${OPS_DIR}/MountDatastore.sh" ] || \
   [ ! -x "${OPS_DIR}/GetFromDatastore.sh" ] || \
   [ ! -x "${OPS_DIR}/PutToDatastore.sh" ]; then
    exec /usr/bin/nala "$@"
fi

# 2. Derive bucket name using ADT config helpers (falls back to a dedicated ADT cache bucket)
if [ -x "${ADT_BASE}/utilities/config/ExtractConfigValue.sh" ]; then
    WEBSITE_URL="$("${ADT_BASE}/utilities/config/ExtractConfigValue.sh" 'WEBSITEURL' 2>/dev/null || true)"
fi

if [ -n "${NALA_CACHE_BUCKET:-}" ]; then
    CACHE_BUCKET="${NALA_CACHE_BUCKET}"
elif [ -n "${WEBSITE_URL:-}" ]; then
    # Aligns with ADT datastore nomenclature: <website_url>-<purpose>
    SANITIZED_URL="$(printf '%s' "${WEBSITE_URL}" | /usr/bin/tr '.' '-')"
    CACHE_BUCKET="${SANITIZED_URL}-nala-cache"
else
    CACHE_BUCKET="adt-shared-nala-cache"
fi

# Namespace objects inside the bucket by OS codename and architecture
OS_CODENAME="$(. /etc/os-release 2>/dev/null && printf '%s' "${VERSION_CODENAME:-linux}")"
ARCH="$(/usr/bin/dpkg --print-architecture 2>/dev/null || printf 'amd64')"
BUCKET_PREFIX="${CACHE_BUCKET}/${OS_CODENAME}-${ARCH}"

CACHE_DIR="/var/cache/apt/archives"
PARTIAL_DIR="${CACHE_DIR}/partial"
LOCK_FILE="/var/lock/adt-nala-cache.lock"
WORK_DIR="$(/bin/mktemp -d /tmp/adt-nala-cache.XXXXXX)"

cleanup() {
    /bin/rm -rf "${WORK_DIR}"
}
trap cleanup EXIT

case "${1:-}" in
    install|upgrade|full-upgrade)
        # Prevent concurrent apt/nala lock collisions on the same VM
        if command -v flock >/dev/null 2>&1 && [ "${ADT_NALA_LOCKED:-0}" != "1" ]; then
            export ADT_NALA_LOCKED=1
            exec /usr/bin/flock "${LOCK_FILE}" "$0" "$@"
        fi

        /bin/mkdir -p "${PARTIAL_DIR}"

        # Ensure the datastore bucket exists across configured ADT regions (idempotent)
        "${OPS_DIR}/MountDatastore.sh" "${CACHE_BUCKET}" "distributed" >/dev/null 2>&1 || :

        # 3. Record existing .deb packages in local APT cache
        /bin/ls -1 "${CACHE_DIR}"/*.deb 2>/dev/null | /usr/bin/xargs -r -n1 /usr/bin/basename | /usr/bin/sort > "${WORK_DIR}/before.txt" || :

        # 4. Strip nala-specific flags before querying apt-get for required .deb filenames
        subcmd="$1"; shift
        apt_args=""
        for arg in "$@"; do
            case "$arg" in
                --no-update|--raw-dpkg|--verbose|-v|--simple|--assume-yes|-y) ;;
                *) apt_args="${apt_args} $(printf '%s' "$arg" | /bin/sed "s/'/'\\\\''/g; s/^/'/; s/$/'/")" ;;
            esac
        done

        eval "set -- ${apt_args}"
        /usr/bin/apt-get --print-uris -y "${subcmd}" "$@" 2>/dev/null | /usr/bin/awk '/^'\''/ {print $2}' > "${WORK_DIR}/needed.txt" || :

        # 5. Read-Through Check: Pull missing .deb files from local-region ADT Datastore
        while IFS= read -r deb_file; do
            if [ -n "${deb_file}" ] && [ ! -f "${CACHE_DIR}/${deb_file}" ]; then
                # GetFromDatastore.sh fetches from the primary/local region into PARTIAL_DIR
                if "${OPS_DIR}/GetFromDatastore.sh" "${BUCKET_PREFIX}/${deb_file}" "${PARTIAL_DIR}" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
                    if [ -f "${PARTIAL_DIR}/${deb_file}" ]; then
                        /bin/mv -f "${PARTIAL_DIR}/${deb_file}" "${CACHE_DIR}/${deb_file}"
                        printf "[ADT-CACHE HIT]  %s\n" "${deb_file}"
                    fi
                else
                    printf "[ADT-CACHE MISS] %s\n" "${deb_file}"
                fi
            fi
        done < "${WORK_DIR}/needed.txt"

        # 6. Execute nala (uses cached .deb files and downloads misses from upstream mirrors)
        # Force Keep-Downloaded-Packages so newly fetched .deb files remain available for step 7
        /usr/bin/nala "${subcmd}" -o APT::Keep-Downloaded-Packages="true" "$@"

        # 7. Write-Back: Push newly downloaded .deb files to ADT Datastore (replicated across regions)
        /bin/ls -1 "${CACHE_DIR}"/*.deb 2>/dev/null | /usr/bin/xargs -r -n1 /usr/bin/basename | /usr/bin/sort | /usr/bin/comm -13 "${WORK_DIR}/before.txt" - > "${WORK_DIR}/new.txt" || :

        while IFS= read -r new_deb; do
            if [ -n "${new_deb}" ] && [ -f "${CACHE_DIR}/${new_deb}" ]; then
                printf "[ADT-CACHE PUSH] %s\n" "${new_deb}"
                # "no" = do not delete local source file after upload; "distributed" = sync to all regions
                "${OPS_DIR}/PutToDatastore.sh" "${CACHE_DIR}/${new_deb}" "${BUCKET_PREFIX}" "no" "distributed" >/dev/null 2>&1 || :
            fi
        done < "${WORK_DIR}/new.txt"
        ;;
    *)
        # Pass non-install commands (update, search, history, autopurge) directly to nala
        exec /usr/bin/nala "$@"
        ;;
esac
Use code with caution.

How to Wire This into Your ADT Repositories

To make every machine role in your forked ADT repositories (adt-webserver-scripts, adt-database-scripts, adt-autoscaler-scripts, adt-build-machine-scripts) use this automatically: [1] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/Development/AddingANewDatastoreProvider)
1. Verify GetFromDatastore.sh / PutToDatastore.sh Argument Signatures:
In ADT, ${HOME}/services/datastore/operations/GetFromDatastore.sh and PutToDatastore.sh wrap PerformGetFromDatastore.sh and PerformPutToDatastore.sh. Depending on your ADT branch version:
	• Some versions expect PutToDatastore.sh <local_file> <bucket/prefix> <delete_after:yes|no> <mode:local|distributed>.
	• Others take PutToDatastore.sh <local_file> <bucket/prefix> <mode> (handling deletion separately).
	• Check the top 15 lines of ${HOME}/services/datastore/operations/PutToDatastore.sh and GetFromDatastore.sh in your fork to confirm the exact positional parameters ($1, $2, $3, $4).
2. Install After InitialiseDatastoreConfig.sh:
In ADT's boot sequence (CloudInitWorkflow / InitialiseDatastoreConfig.sh), datastore credentials (/root/.s5cfg-1, ~/.s3cfg, or rclone.conf) are written before application packages (nginx, php, mariadb, postgres, etc.) are installed.
	• Place InstallWithNalaCache.sh in your providerscripts/ or utilities/software/ directory.
	• Update your software installation scripts (or symlink /usr/local/bin/nala -> ${HOME}/utilities/software/InstallWithNalaCache.sh once InitialiseDatastoreConfig.sh finishes) so every subsequent nala install call during server provisioning hits your S3 datastore first. [1] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/Development/AddingANewDatastoreProvider), [2] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/newdoco/Deployment/Config%20Directories%20Synchronisation)
3. Multi-Region Speed Optimization:
Because ADT orders S3_HOST_BASE with the local region first (--credentials-file /root/.s5cfg-1), GetFromDatastore.sh will always read from the datacenter region closest to the VM being provisioned, while PutToDatastore.sh ... distributed populates all secondary regions in the background.
If you'd like to fine-tune the script to match your exact ADT fork, let me know:
• Which datastore provider tool you have configured in ADT (s5cmd, s3cmd, or rclone) so we can batch transfers if supported
• Whether you want to auto-purge /var/cache/apt/archives/*.deb after PutToDatastore.sh completes to keep small VPS root disks clean
• The exact header/arguments of your fork's GetFromDatastore.sh if it differs from the standard 2-argument signature
